   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Vowels and consonants",
     "texto": [
      "The letters **ś** and **ć** sound soft; **sz** sounds like «sh» and **cz** like «ch».",
      "The **ł** is close to English «w»; **ń** sounds like «ny» in «canyon». **Ą** and **ę** are nasal; the final **ę** usually sounds almost like «e»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Stress and greetings",
     "texto": [
      "In most Polish words of several syllables, the stress falls on the next-to-last syllable.",
      "**Dzień dobry** is a formal greeting. **Cześć** is used to greet and say goodbye informally; **Do widzenia** is a more formal goodbye."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Formal address",
     "texto": [
      "**Pan** is used to address a man formally, and **pani** a woman. Careful: **pan** does not mean the English «pan» (the cooking pot). It is a false friend.",
      "Among people you know well, people ask **Jak się masz?**, literally «how do you have yourself?»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
